  "reconstruction": {
    "text": "For every complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space E equipped with its compatible Borel measurable structure and canonical Haar/Lebesgue measure λ = volume, every real-valued potential U : E → ℝ of class C², and every pair α, β of nonnegative real constants with α > 0, suppose that for all x, v ∈ E,\n    α ‖v‖² ≤ D²U(x)[v,v] ≤ β ‖v‖².\nHere α and β are the real coercions of their nonnegative-real inputs, and D²U(x)[v,v] is the iterated Fréchet derivative evaluated twice on v.\nDefine μ = λ.tilted(-U). In this definition the exponential density exp(-U) is normalized against λ when it is integrable; otherwise the tilt is the zero measure. Thus, on the integrable branch, μ(dx) = Z⁻¹ exp(-U(x)) λ(dx), where Z = ∫ exp(-U(x)) λ(dx). This displayed measure and its probability status are not input assumptions.\nThe conclusions, jointly, are: μ is a probability measure; the Hilbert gradient ∇U is Bochner integrable with respect to μ; and\n    ∫_E ∇U(x) μ(dx) = 0_E.\nThe gradient is the real Hilbert Riesz representative of DU, so DU(x)[v] = ⟨∇U(x),v⟩. The integral is an E-valued Bochner integral and its first integrability is a proved output. There is no assumption α ≤ β, β > 0, normalization of exp(-U), or positive dimension. When dim E = 0, E contains only 0_E, all directional inequalities have zero on both sides, and the gradient and its mean are zero; α > β is allowed in this case.",
